{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's a reality check for Huntsville homeowners: if your home was built before 2015, there's a good chance your gutters are ready for replacement. Those builder-grade sectional gutters that came with your house? They were chosen because they were cheap, not because they were right for North Alabama's climate.</p>

<p>Huntsville's rapid growth through the 2000s and 2010s meant homes went up fast. Subdivisions in Hampton Cove, Big Cove, Jones Valley, and throughout south Huntsville were built during a construction boom when builders cut corners on gutters. The result: a generation of Huntsville homes with undersized 5-inch gutters, hangers spaced 32 inches apart (code minimum), and .027 gauge aluminum that dents if you look at it wrong. Those systems fail within 7-10 years — leaking at seams, pulling away from fascia boards, overflowing during every moderate rainstorm.</p>

<p>Combine failing gutters with Madison County's infamous red clay soil (which doesn't absorb water) and the region's 55+ inches of annual rainfall, and you've got a recipe for foundation problems, landscape erosion, and water damage. Seamless gutters solve this at the source — fewer seams means fewer leaks, and custom fabrication means gutters that actually fit your home and handle the water volume you need.</p>

<p><strong>A word on pricing:</strong> You'll find companies quoting $4-6 per linear foot for sectional gutters. That's a different product. Seamless gutters cost more — but they last twice as long, leak far less, and actually handle Huntsville's rainfall. Cheap gutters are expensive in the long run.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are fabricated from a single piece of aluminum coil — rolled on-site by a portable machine we bring to your driveway. Instead of 10-foot sections joined with seams every 10 feet, you get continuous runs with seams only at corners and downspout connections.</p>

<p>Why does this matter? Every seam in a gutter system is a future failure point. Sealant degrades. Joints flex and separate. Debris catches at the overlap. Sectional gutters have seams every 10 feet; a 50-foot run has five potential leak points. Seamless eliminates all five.</p>

<p><strong>What we actually install:</strong> 6-inch K-style seamless gutters in .032 gauge aluminum with hidden hangers at 24-inch spacing. We size downspouts (3x4, not 2x3) and position them every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run. Everything is fabricated to your exact measurements — we don't cut generic sections to fit.</p>

<p><strong>The honest part:</strong> "Seamless" is a slight exaggeration. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout outlets. What you don't have is mid-run seams — and that's what actually fails. The corner seams are sealed and rarely cause problems.</p>
{% endset %}

